---
import Variant from '../../layouts/Variant.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Variant
  title="ExCSV sidecar"
  description="Detached ExCSV metadata for an existing CSV or TSV file — schema, SQL, and aggregations without duplicating data rows."
  heading="Sidecar"
  lede="Keep plain CSV/TSV for tools that only understand delimited text. Ship schema, SQL, and aggregations in a sibling metadata file."
>
  <h2>Pairing</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metadata (sidecar)</th><th>Data file</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>sales.excsv</code></td><td><code>sales.csv</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>sales.extsv</code></td><td><code>sales.tsv</code> (SHOULD use <code>delim=tab</code>)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Same basename, different extension. The sidecar holds <strong>only</strong> the <code>#!excsv</code> header and <code>#</code> meta lines — no data section.</p>

  <h2>Required header field</h2>
  <p>
    A sidecar <strong>MUST</strong> set <code>reference=&lt;path&gt;</code> on the <code>#!excsv</code> line.
    The path is relative to the sidecar file's directory, MUST NOT be absolute, and names the data file
    (typically a basename such as <code>sales.csv</code>).
  </p>

  <CodeBlock title="Sidecar + data sibling" description="sales.excsv — metadata only.">
{`#!excsv version=0.3 delim=comma header=1 rows=2 reference=sales.csv
#@source: sales_db.orders
#@exported: 2026-03-24T12:00:00Z
#column name=id type=int unique=1
#column name=customer type=string required=1
#column name=amount type=decimal unit=USD
#$ddl: CREATE TABLE orders (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer TEXT, amount NUMERIC(8,2))
#%count_nonnull: 2,2,2
#%sum: ,,750.50`}
  </CodeBlock>

  <CodeBlock title="sales.csv — ordinary CSV" description="No ExCSV header; sidecar supplies dialect and schema.">
{`id,customer,amount
1,Acme Corp,500.00
2,Globex Inc,250.50`}
  </CodeBlock>

  <h2>Derived fields</h2>
  <p>
    <code>rows=</code>, <code>checksum=</code>, and <code>#%</code> lines describe the <strong>referenced</strong> data file,
    not the sidecar bytes. Validating a checksum requires opening the pair. Metadata-only tools may parse the sidecar alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Discovery</h2>
  <p>When a consumer opens <code>sales.csv</code>, it <strong>MAY</strong> look for <code>sales.excsv</code> in the same directory; when opening <code>sales.tsv</code>, it <strong>MAY</strong> look for <code>sales.extsv</code>. When opening a sidecar, resolve <code>reference=</code> to load rows.</p>

  <h2>Errors</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sidecar with <code>reference=</code> and any data row → <strong>MUST</strong> fail</li>
    <li>Sidecar without <code>reference=</code> → invalid as sidecar (may still be a header-only stub if no data)</li>
    <li><code>.extsv</code> with <code>delim=comma</code> → <strong>SHOULD</strong> warn</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>vs <code>#@source</code></h2>
  <p><code>#@source</code> is provenance (which system or table produced the export). <code>reference=</code> is structural: which file on disk holds the rows.</p>

  <h2>Related: <a href="https://github.com/MetaCSV/MetaCSV">MetaCSV</a></h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/MetaCSV/MetaCSV">MetaCSV</a> (draft) uses <code>data.csv</code> + <code>data.mcsv</code> where the sidecar is a small CSV of
    <code>domain,key,value</code> rows (<code>file</code>/<code>csv</code> dialect, <code>data,col/n/type</code> for columns).
    ExCSV sidecars share the goal but keep one meta language (<code>#!excsv</code>, <code>#column</code>, <code>#%</code>, <code>#$</code>) and explicit <code>reference=</code>.
  </p>
  <p>See the <a href="/spec/#prior-art">MetaCSV comparison</a> in Prior Art for a field-by-field mapping and translation notes.</p>

  <p><a href="/spec/#sidecar">Spec §2.2</a> · <a href="/examples/">Examples</a></p>
</Variant>
